{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q9ZFV2","uniProtkbId":"EUTA_SALTY","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2000-05-30","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2025-06-18","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1999-05-01","entryVersion":103,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":3.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Salmonella typhimurium (strain LT2 / SGSC1412 / ATCC 700720)","taxonId":99287,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Salmonella"]},"proteinExistence":"2: Evidence at transcript level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Ethanolamine ammonia-lyase reactivase EutA"}}},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"eutA"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"STM2459"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P76551"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15516577"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"Reactivates suicidally inhibited ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL), cyanocobalamin-inactivated EAL and O(2)-inactivated EAL; requires Mg(2+), ATP and adenosylcobalamin. Reactivation probably occurs by the ATP-dependent exchange of cobalamin (By similarity). Protects EAL from inhibition by CN-B12, does not have adenosylation activity (Probable) (PubMed:15516577)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29531136"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Expression of the eut operon allows this bacteria to use ethanolamine as a carbon, nitrogen and energy source. It relies on cobalamin (vitamin B12) both as a cofactor for the ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL) activity and to induce the operon (PubMed:3045078). EA enhances bacterial survival in macrophages in a concentration-dependent manner, suggesting it is an important nutrient during infection (PubMed:29531136)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Bacterial microcompartment","id":"SL-0544"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Part of the 17-gene eut operon transcribed from a single promoter, induced by ethanolamine and adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl, vitamin B12)"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15516577"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16585748"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"Can use ethanolamine (EA) as a nitrogen source when AdoCbl but not cyano-B12 or hydroxy-B12 is provided; cannot use EA as a carbon source (PubMed:15516577, PubMed:2656649). Required for aerobic growth on ethanolamine supplemented with cobalamin (vitamin B12) (PubMed:10464203). A non-polar deletion mutant does not grow on EA between pH 5.5 and pH 8.5 (PubMed:16585748)"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the EutA family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":467,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ethanolamine ammonia-lyase reactivase EutA","featureId":"PRO_0000087080"}],"keywords":[{"id":"KW-1283","category":"Cellular component","name":"Bacterial microcompartment"},{"id":"KW-0143","category":"Molecular function","name":"Chaperone"},{"id":"KW-1185","c